n afghanistan  so here the incentive is clear  already 62 billion were offered to the  taliban for uh various projects now most  nations involved in these talks felina  particularly china russia and pakistan  have concerns especially to do with  terrorism emanating from afghan soil how  do they plan to address them with the  taliban  well in two possible ways the one of  course direct by offering them  incentives by incorporating afghanistan  into geo-economic projects uh  china's also seeks to uh relaunch mining  activities uh they want to  let the taliban take care of the  security problems the indirect way the  second way is by cooperating with the  central asian countries and specifically  by russia china will meet russia as a  security provider on the ground which is  uh by the way happening russia also did  not leave the country last summer and in  fact is  is a kind of uh engaged in a modus  vivendi with china to deal with the  afghanistan problem it's a diplomatic  boost for moscow but it's also a huge  security boost for china to deal with  these problems collectively so jointly  so that means of course also using  regional organizations such as the  shanghai cooperation organization to  involve the central asian republics and  of course they will engage with pakistan  pakistan has  been offered a lot of economic  incentives by both  russia and china and of course pakistan  has a direct influence on afghanistan  and of course it is that close  cooperation between russia and china  that we also want to look at uh  villeneuve because foreign ministers of  both these nations sage lavrov and bangi  met on wednesday and reaffirmed the no  limits partnership that their leaders  had agreed to in february let's just  quickly hear what sage lavrov had to say  during this meeting  we are living through a very serious  stage in the history of international  relations  i am convinced that at the end of this  stage the international situation will  be much clearer and t